\DOC LOCAL_MUL_5_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_5_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Applies a pre-proved subroutine-correctness lemma for a 5-word modular
multiplication step, advancing the symbolic machine state in an {eventually}
goal past that code block and recording the multiplication's result as new
assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} ({0} in all observed traces) selecting an
offset/variant within the lemma application; the second is a {string list}
giving the name of the result variable followed by the names of the two
operands of the multiplication, fixing the names used in the newly introduced
assumptions and in the stated congruence. The goal's conclusion must be an
{eventually} statement about a current state variable; it is replaced by the
same statement about a fresh successor state variable, otherwise unchanged:
{
   A, pc-at-old-offset, preserved-reads-at-s, MAYCHANGE frame, flag-reads-at-s ?- eventually ... s ...
   ========================================================================================  LOCAL_MUL_5_TAC 0 ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
   A, pc-at-new-offset, preserved-reads-at-s', extended MAYCHANGE frame,
   read dest-buffer s' = res, bound on res, res == op1 * op2 (mod p) ?- eventually ... s' ...
}
\noindent Here all assumptions mentioning the old state are discharged and
replaced by corresponding ones about the new state: the program-counter
assumption is updated to the new code offset, the preserved register/memory
reads are re-stated at the new state, the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame is
extended with the extra components the block writes, and three assumptions are
added — a read giving the destination buffer's new contents as the named result
variable, a size bound on it, and a congruence relating it to the product of
the two named operands modulo the field prime. Assumptions not referring to the
state (side conditions, nonoverlapping facts, definitions) are kept unchanged,
and flag-read assumptions about the old state are dropped.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} x86 statement.

\ENDDOC
